m25.content:
The buggy method is `org.jfree.chart.plot.PiePlot.initialise(java.awt.Graphics2D, java.awt.geom.Rectangle2D, org.jfree.chart.plot.PiePlot, java.lang.Integer, org.jfree.chart.plot.PlotRenderingInfo)`.

Diagnosis:
`PiePlot3D.draw()` is intended to tolerate a `null` dataset and already has a guard at lines 331-338:

```java
if (DatasetUtilities.isEmptyOrNull(getDataset())) {
    drawNoDataMessage(...);
    return;
}
```

But it calls `initialise(...)` earlier at line 280. Inside `PiePlot.initialise()`, the code unconditionally executes:

```java
state.setTotal(DatasetUtilities.calculatePieDatasetTotal(plot.getDataset()));
```

`DatasetUtilities.calculatePieDatasetTotal(...)` throws `IllegalArgumentException` when the dataset is `null`. Dynamic verification confirmed that in the failing test, `plot.getDataset()` is `null`, and execution throws from `PiePlot.initialise()` before `PiePlot3D.draw()` reaches its null-dataset handling branch.

So the root cause is an ordering/defensive-check bug in `PiePlot.initialise()`: it assumes a non-null dataset in a code path where `null` is expected to be supported during drawing.
